      "text": "To find the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\), we first need to determine the repeating decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Perform the Long Division\n\n1. **Long Division of 5 by 14:**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccc}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.} & \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\\\\n\\cline{2-5}\n14 & 5.000000 \\\\\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{4.2} \\\\\n\\cline{2-3}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.75} \\\\\n\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe division process reveals that the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(0.\\overline{357142}\\). This means the decimal repeats every 6 digits: \\(357142\\).\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Position within the Cycle\n\nWe need to find the position of the 1314th digit within the repeating block. To do this, we find the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6 (the length of the repeating sequence).\n\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\n\nA remainder of 0 means that the 1314th digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block \\(357142\\).\n\n### Step 3: Identify the 1314th Digit\n\nThe repeating block is \\(357142\\). The last digit of this block is \\(2\\).\n\nThus, the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
